# KNOBS-render11.env — orin 22, the render11 candidate's build environment.
#
# Written FROM THE BANNER of the run that produced ../stage11/render11-candidate, not from memory.
# The verb was `./arroyo esp-jetson` with NO knob set at all: no UNAOS_SDMMCROOT (that knob was
# DELETED this arc — b0536d83 — the root is now the disk the kernel was FOUND on, by content), and
# nothing else on the command line.
#
# Banner, quoted verbatim from the staging run (gate-espjetson2.log):
#   ⚡ kernel features: ehcihid,kbdwit,sdhcblk,smolnet
#   ⚡ kernel features (jetson): ehcihid,kbdwit,sdhcblk,smolnet,tegra,tegrasmp,bsptick,bsprun,sdmmc
#
# NOTE THE TWO THINGS THIS FILE IS ASSERTING BY BEING EMPTY OF THEM:
#   * `sdmmc` IS in the jetson feature list, and it got there by DEFAULT — b1885dbc made it
#     default-on for the jetson media path. There is no `#require=sdmmc` line here and there must
#     not be one: a require would describe a knob that is no longer a knob.
#   * `UNAOS_SDMMCROOT` appears nowhere. The knob does not exist. If a later playbook sets it, the
#     build will not fail — it will simply be ignored, which is the more dangerous shape, so it is
#     called out here rather than left to be discovered on a bench.
#
# TO REPRODUCE THE CANDIDATE:
#   cd <worktree>/unaos && ./arroyo esp-jetson
# then compare `strings -a target/aarch64_esp/kernel.elf | grep UNAOS-BUILD-STAMP-1:` against
# BUILD-INFO.txt's build_sha. A CLEAN tree at the same commit yields byte-identical output; a dirty
# one stamps `-d<6hex>` and will NOT match, which is the point of the suffix (VERSIONWIN, 907435cd).

# (no environment variables are set for this build)

UNAOS_BUILD_COMMIT=600887c2347f7ba33da6d1c3a302be256eb74dec
UNAOS_BUILD_STAMP=600887c2
UNAOS_KERNEL_ELF_SHA256=6dbeda7d1ae8e942fb764e13a175168e1d8a10e235abf0ef9ee5fd9d15c58c9d
